WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.740
Welcome back to The David Pakman Show. It's
great to welcome to the program today, Nicholas

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:08.500
Christakis, Sterling, professor of social
natural science at Yale University and also

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.510
author of Apollo's Arrow.
It's so great to have you on. I'm glad to

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:18.730
be talking to you today. David, thank you
for having me. So there are so many things

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:26.090
that we can say about the medical handling
of this global hundred year pandemic, about

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:31.410
governmental failures or successes, vaccine
development, et cetera. But one of the things

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:37.339
that I find interesting, which you've talked
about, are sort of the cultural aspects, what

00:00:37.339 --> 00:00:43.019
we learned and what was exposed maybe about
American culture over the last nearly year

00:00:43.019 --> 00:00:47.769
of dealing with the global pandemic. Can you
kind of frame that conversation for us and

00:00:47.769 --> 00:00:52.159
explain what what we should be thinking about
when we think about culture and the pandemic?

00:00:52.159 --> 00:00:56.659
Right, well, I think you already highlighted
one important factor, which is something I

00:00:56.659 --> 00:01:01.339
would lead with right away, which is this
way that we have come to live in the last

00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:06.630
year. And a lot of the things that have happened,
for example, the death of health care workers

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:11.740
or a lot of the denial and the lies that we
have seen about the pandemic, many, many of

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.490
these features seem so unnatural and alien.
But it's important to understand that plagues

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:22.429
are not new to our species. They're just new
to us. We think all of these things are so

00:01:22.429 --> 00:01:27.450
unusual and weird and unnatural and how can
we? But that's just not true. And in fact,

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:34.700
when you study the psychological, social and
economic responses to the covid-19 pandemic,

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:41.150
you see that many of those responses actually
have obtained with pandemics and plagues going

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:45.840
back thousands of years. So let me give you
a couple of examples. Let me start with a

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.860
really simple one, which is the death of health
care workers. Many people are aware of the

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:53.859
fact that many hundreds of American health
care workers and thousands of health care

00:01:53.859 --> 00:01:58.499
workers around the world have died caring
for patients who are sick. In fact, one of

00:01:58.499 --> 00:02:03.549
the reasons we should behave better is to
reduce our imposing risk on these people of

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:09.170
dying, caring for us. Well, facilities in
the plague of Athens and 430 B.C. talks about

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:15.030
the death death of health care workers. Pope
Clement, the sixth in the bubonic plague in

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:20.299
13 47, talks about the death of nurses taking
care of patients. This is not new or lies

00:02:20.299 --> 00:02:24.800
in denial. We've seen huge amounts of like
superstitions about, you know, you could use

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.730
bleach to treat the condition or or or like,
you know, Bill Gates is going to inject us.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:36.849
The vaccine has microchips is ridiculous or
or denial, including at the former president.

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:39.989
The White House saying it's going away, there's
nothing happening, it's going away, which

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:46.790
was completely false and known to be false.
This also this response is typical of human

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:52.030
beings during times of plague. Because we
don't want to believe that this bad thing

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:56.060
is happening to us and on and on, I could
go on. I mean, blame, for example, the desire

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:01.409
to blame others is, again, very typical. We
we saw this during bubonic plague when there

00:03:01.409 --> 00:03:06.709
was anti-Semitism. You know, the Jews were
blamed. We saw it during HIV. You know, when

00:03:06.709 --> 00:03:11.910
when gay people were blamed or Haitians were
blamed or IV drug users were blamed. There's

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:16.090
always this desire to blame someone else when
there's a time of plague. Anyway, I could

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:19.819
go on. But there are these these things that
are happening to us or I'll say one more thing

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:26.659
and then I'll shut up. You know, the you know,
there's a lot of misunderstanding about what

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:32.090
is happening to us in terms of the economy,
for example. Yes. So people think that the

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:36.270
economy is collapsing because of what the
state what the government is doing to us.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:40.700
But if you read accounts of plague's going
back thousands of years, for example, John,

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:46.650
of Ephesus, a priest writing about the plague
of Justinian in 500 A.D., talks about how

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:51.689
the economy collapses. Everything slowed and
ceased. People were not out and about. There

00:03:51.689 --> 00:03:55.410
was no government that was ordering schools
or businesses to close in that period. It's

00:03:55.410 --> 00:04:00.609
just this is what the virus does. It's it
stops social interactions, which therefore

00:04:00.609 --> 00:04:06.560
stops the economy. So all of these things
that we're seeing. Ah, a once in a century

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.980
event, and we just, I think, have to accept
that and maturely confront it.

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:18.760
You know, I I interviewed Brian Greene during
the pandemic earlier in the pandemic. And

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:23.940
one of the things he pointed out was that
he thinks there's a certain naivete to thinking

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:30.000
that if we were to make contact with intelligent
alien species, all of our our earthly problems

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.730
would all of a sudden seem sort of quaint
and irrelevant. And we would come together

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:40.700
as a as a global people because now this thing
that transcends our problems has taken place.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:45.760
He thinks even that is probably naive to think
that there would be some initial instinct

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.420
of that. And very quickly, we would go back
to our normal problems. Is there a similar

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:56.190
naivety? And I think the answer clearly is
yes, as evidenced by the last many months,

00:04:56.190 --> 00:05:01.290
to think that something like this would have
allowed us to cross the partizan bickering

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:07.780
and not to devolve into the same sort of arguments,
the politically aligned arguments that we

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:11.810
see with abortion and taxes and so many of
these other things. Is it just fundamentally

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:15.480
naive to think that something like a global
pandemic would cross those bridges?

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:20.360
Well, first of all, let me back up and say
there's a very famous exchange between I think

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:28.430
it was President Ronald Reagan and and Mikhail
Gorbachev where Reagan says to they're having

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.192
an exchange about how they can work together.
And Reagan says, well, let me ask you, you

00:05:32.192 --> 00:05:36.670
know, the Earth was invaded by aliens. Would
you come to our defense? And Gorbachev says,

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:42.650
of course, you know, and so this this trope,
this idea that's in science fiction movies,

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:50.790
that human beings would come together to confront
a shared threat. Is actually deeply fundamental,

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:55.280
and there's another very famous social psychology
experiment or demonstration called the Robber's

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:02.590
Cave Demonstration, which was taken, which
took place in the 1950s, where 12, 11 or 12

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:08.850
year old boys were taken into a summer camp
and divided into two groups. Yeah, and and

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:12.620
then they were their group solidarity was
fostered. And then it was revealed that there

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:17.670
was this other group nearby and then group
competition was fostered. And then the investigators

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:22.820
contrive to have a shared threat and show
that now, having created these divisions,

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:26.580
they could undo the divisions. The shared
threat was they contrived to have the water

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:31.230
supply to the camp interrupted. So the boys
had to work together across groups, you know,

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:36.630
to fix the problem. So so there's this all
this background on exactly the topic that

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:41.890
you're mentioning. And it is true that a shared
threat can bind groups together. And many

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:46.600
people have for years thought that a virus
or a germ would have that effect. In other

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:51.960
words, we all are human. We're all facing
this deadly threat. We should work together

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:58.100
to confront it. But there are a few features
of play of epidemics and this one in particular

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:01.810
that make that hard.
My guess is my guess is one is that not everybody

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:07.740
agrees as to the nature of the threat first.
And partly that's because this this germ,

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:13.050
serious and deadly though it is, is not as
deadly as thank God it could have been. Just

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:17.510
to be clear, we could have been facing a bubonic
plague type situation right now on the planet.

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:21.130
And many people you need to people need to
understand this is just by the luck of God,

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:25.340
that we're not facing something that's 10
or 30 times deadlier like in the movie Contagion.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.970
Anyway, this particular pathogen is serious.
It kills about one percent of the people that

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:34.930
get symptoms from it. But it's not so deadly
as smallpox or bubonic plague, which I think

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:39.540
would have galvanized public interest and
maybe helped us transcend some of our political

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:46.710
divisions. First point, second. The germ struck
us at a particularly vulnerable moment in

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:52.500
the life of our society in terms of our intellectual
life. We have as a nation lost respect for

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:57.720
science. We have we have politicized the notion
of expertize we think that experts are elites

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:01.890
who are trying to take advantage of us rather
than just experts. You know, like if you have

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:05.150
a car problem, I don't think I'm as good as
my car mechanic and figuring out what the

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:08.800
problem is. I want to pay someone who knows
more than I do about the problem. And if you

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.081
have a surgeon or anything else, there's expertize
in the world or a plumbing disaster, there's

00:08:13.081 --> 00:08:18.060
expertize in the world. So but we have begun
to politicize this notion of expertize to

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:23.140
our detriment, we have high levels of political
polarization and economic inequality already

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:28.100
a century high levels when the virus struck.
And finally, we have lost the capacity for

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:34.060
nuance in our civil civic discourse. People
say, oh, you're with me or you're against

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:38.639
me or things are black or white. But adults
know that that's not true. Things are not

00:08:38.639 --> 00:08:43.509
black or white. Most things are shades of
gray. Anyway, all of those things, plus the

00:08:43.509 --> 00:08:48.930
relatively low but still high level of death
from this germ have made it difficult for

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:53.259
us to band together to confront it. Plus,
I think, frankly, incompetent leadership in

00:08:53.259 --> 00:08:58.610
the former administration at the White House,
which minimized the threat and did not act

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:02.811
to protect the public interest. I'll say one
more thing and then I'll shut up again. Many

00:09:02.811 --> 00:09:07.129
people say, well, OK, Trump didn't do so well.
But, you know, what about England or Italy

00:09:07.129 --> 00:09:11.810
or other countries? It's true. Many other
countries also did poorly, but many countries

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:17.040
did well. And not just rich islands like Taiwan
and New Zealand. Germany did well. Greece

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:21.800
did well. You know where the United States
of America. I expected more from us. I expected

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:26.550
us to do well in confronting this threat.
Yeah. Rwanda is also an interesting example,

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:31.100
actually, of a country that that that did
quite well, certainly better than many might

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:35.910
have imagined if they didn't know that much
about about Rwanda. When we think about the

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:41.050
next year, a lot of the discussions are when
will herd immunity be reached? But there's

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:45.140
some pretty interesting pieces that suggest
that the what might be called the cultural

00:09:45.140 --> 00:09:50.810
end to the pandemic will come before the medical
end of the pandemic, particularly if the most

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:56.200
at risk are vaccinated first, as is happening
and the deaths go down dramatically. So there's

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:00.709
kind of a question as to whether the cultural
and the medical ends will coincide. What are

00:10:00.709 --> 00:10:06.589
you looking for in terms of this next year
when you look at the cultural impact, the

00:10:06.589 --> 00:10:10.850
rollout of vaccinations, the transition to
a new federal administration handling this?

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:16.160
Well, I think you're highlighting the key
issues. There's this notion of herd immunity,

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:21.089
which is this idea that a population can be
immune to a condition or immune to having

00:10:21.089 --> 00:10:25.639
an epidemic of the condition, even if not
everyone within the population is immune.

00:10:25.639 --> 00:10:30.800
Let's understand. And and through various
calculations and making an accommodation for

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:35.350
something known as the network effects, probably
at least 50 percent of Americans have to have

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:39.410
acquired immunity to the condition before
we get herd immunity. Now, when that happens,

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:43.709
the virus is not gone. It still circulates
and can kill people. But we've taken the wind

00:10:43.709 --> 00:10:49.089
out of its sails. Its epidemic force has been
nullified. When we get to this critical threshold

00:10:49.089 --> 00:10:54.620
now, we have miraculously invented a vaccine
where the first generation of humans to confront

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:59.879
the threat of plagues that has been capable
to develop a specific countermeasure in real

00:10:59.879 --> 00:11:04.990
time. It's amazing. But as we are all seeing
in the news, we have to manufacture hundreds

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:10.389
of millions of doses, distribute them, which
is not easy, and persuade people to take them.

00:11:10.389 --> 00:11:14.930
And I think it'll take at least a year before
we get to 50 percent or more, which is what

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:19.940
we really need. Vaccination at least 50 percent.
Meanwhile, the virus is still spreading. About

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:25.999
14 percent of Americans are infected. So far.
This has a long runway. So either way, I think

00:11:25.999 --> 00:11:30.759
it's going to take another year before we
reach herd immunity. And during that time,

00:11:30.759 --> 00:11:35.990
we're going to have to live in a changed world
with wearing masks, with periodic school and

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:40.149
business closures, with gathering bands, with
extra hygiene and all of the headaches that

00:11:40.149 --> 00:11:45.470
we've come to be familiar with. And this is
before the emergence of new, more communicable

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:51.110
strains, which is another complication. So
I think at least a year and then we'll enter

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:55.999
the the intermediate period where. Some of
the things that you mentioned will begin to

00:11:55.999 --> 00:12:00.279
obtain, which is we're going to have to begin
to confront the psychological, social and

00:12:00.279 --> 00:12:06.070
economic aftershocks, because let's not forget,
tens of millions of Americans are out of work.

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:11.329
Millions of people have lost, businesses have
closed. Millions of children have lost schooling.

00:12:11.329 --> 00:12:15.970
There's a disability many several hundred,
probably two and a half million Americans

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:21.019
will be disabled by this condition. Between
half a million and a million will die. So

00:12:21.019 --> 00:12:26.110
we're going to have a lot of recovery to do
and I think it'll take a while. But come 2020,

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:30.470
for approximately these are approximate, I
think we'll finally enter the post pandemic

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:35.700
period. And then I think it'll be like the
roaring 20s of the 20th century after the

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:40.550
1918 influenza pandemic. We'll have a roaring
20s at the end of the 21st century.

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:44.059
Yeah, one of the things that I've not seen
discussed that much, which I think is important

00:12:44.059 --> 00:12:48.970
and interesting, is I never believed at the
time when in May Donald Trump was saying the

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:52.769
measures that some want you to take are more
damaging than the virus itself. I thought

00:12:52.769 --> 00:12:59.019
that was dangerous and a disaster. But I do
think that there is an aspect of this, which

00:12:59.019 --> 00:13:04.450
is after a year or a year and a half or however
long it is of this feeling that the world

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:09.699
outside of your house is hostile and dangerous
to you. Seeing two people close to each other

00:13:09.699 --> 00:13:14.600
with no masks looks like an act of something
more than what it used to be. Right. This

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:20.459
stuff becomes ingrained. That doesn't get
undone in a month or in two months. And I'm

00:13:20.459 --> 00:13:25.949
curious as to what the long term group psychology
impacts of that is going to be.

00:13:25.949 --> 00:13:31.589
That's exactly right. That's what I was suggesting,
that when we get to early 2022, we'll finally

00:13:31.589 --> 00:13:36.069
have put the biological and epidemiological
impact of the virus behind us. But there's

00:13:36.069 --> 00:13:39.410
still going to be a lot of psychological and
other recovery that will take a couple of

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:44.269
years, judging again from also the history
of past epidemics. Just like you said, not

00:13:44.269 --> 00:13:48.029
everyone's going to take off their mask a
year from now. Right. For a variety of reasons,

00:13:48.029 --> 00:13:50.720
people are going to still be wearing masks.
Some will be cautious. The virus will still

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:56.220
be around. Other people will have immunodeficiency.
Some people will have habits, they'll be worried,

00:13:56.220 --> 00:14:01.069
et cetera. So it's not going to be instantaneous,
nor will any of the other recovery be instantaneous.

00:14:01.069 --> 00:14:05.209
It's going to be a long and enduring shock.
I think what people need to understand is,

00:14:05.209 --> 00:14:10.430
is we happen to be alive like our grandparents
were alive when the world was at war. Right.

00:14:10.430 --> 00:14:15.120
Like when I talked to my Greek relatives in
Greece about this in their 80s. Their frame

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:20.610
of reference is not like the typical American.
It's it's like a European who's used to having

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:25.690
invading armies. My my father remembers the
Nazis invading Athens when he was a boy. So

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:31.079
for him, you know, that was like a once in
a century event. And now this is another once

00:14:31.079 --> 00:14:36.660
in a century event, you know, so so I think
people need to understand that. That this

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:41.980
is a quite a serious and unusual experience
to us, again, for humans have been, you know,

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:46.410
plagues are in the Bible there and Shakespeare
there and Homer there in servants. You know,

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:50.820
there's nothing new about plagues, persay,
but for us, it's quite a new experience. And

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:56.149
we have to understand, much as we wish that
this had not happened to us and much as we

00:14:56.149 --> 00:15:02.490
wish that it would end quickly, it's not it's
just not what plagues do.

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:08.089
We've been speaking with Nicholas Christakis,
who is Sterling, professor of social natural

00:15:08.089 --> 00:15:13.519
science at Yale University, also author of
Apollo's Arrow. I so appreciate your time

00:15:13.519 --> 00:15:15.740
today. David, thank you so much for having
me.

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:15.879
The David Pakman Show at David Pakman Dotcom.

